<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Ballad · 1885 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Botany Bay</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song about convicts sent from Britain to Australia long ago. Australian children still sing it.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>an old air, arranged by Wilhelm Meyer Lutz</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the London burlesque stage; later Australian folk singing</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Gaiety Theatre, London, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1885 (in the burlesque Little Jack Sheppard)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Ballad</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional words to an old air, first known from the London stage in 1885)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Botany Bay&quot; can be traced back to Little Jack Sheppard, a musical burlesque staged at the Gaiety <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1885 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> in 1886. The show was written by Henry Pottinger Stephens and William Yardley, with music composed and arranged by Wilhelm Meyer Lutz. Its programme credits &quot;Botany Bay&quot; as an &quot;Old Air arr. Lutz&quot;. Sheet music from Allan &amp; Co. in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> instead credits Florian Pascal, the pseudonym of Joseph Williams Jr. (1847–1923), the music publisher and composer who published the show's music; Pascal wrote other numbers in the score but was not credited for this one in the programme.</p><p>The song's earlier history is less clear. Its verses share lines with &quot;Farewell to Judges and Juries&quot;, performed in 1820. In 1890 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> paper The Era described the melody as written over a hundred years before, and it appears to have been adapted from the folk song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mus">Mush</a>, Mush&quot;. An older song called &quot;Botany Bay&quot;, catalogued by the British Library as from the 1780s, has no obvious connection: it is about Cook's landing, not the convicts.</p><p>Botany Bay was the place chosen for the settlement of the First Fleet, which brought convicts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in the 18th century. The song looks back on the late 18th and 19th centuries, when the British government transported convicts to the Australian penal colonies for seven-year terms as an alternative to prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. By the time it was written, transportation to New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> had already ended, in 1840, 45 years earlier.</p><p>After Little Jack Sheppard the song became a popular folk song, sung and recorded by Burl Ives and many others. It has no official words, and singers vary them slightly. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> it appears on compilations of children's songs, and documentaries on the transportation of convicts use it. It is sung in the film The Sundowners (1960) and by shearers in the television miniseries The Thorn Birds (1983).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of a convict transported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to the penal colony at Botany Bay. It tells of life on the convict ships and ends by warning young people at home not to steal. The mood is rueful, carried by a singalong refrain.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell to old England for ever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell to my rum coes as well,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Farewell to the well-known Old Bailey</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where I used for to cut such a swell.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Singing too-ral-li, oo-ral-li, addity,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Singing too-ral-li, oo-ral-li, ay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Singing too-ral-li, oo-ral-li, addity,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we're bound for Botany Bay.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There's the captain as is our commander,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There's the bo'sun and all the ship's crew,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There's the first- and the second-class passengers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Knows what we poor convicts go through.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Taint leaving old England we cares about,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Taint cos we mis-spells what we knows,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But because all we light-fingered gentry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hops around with a log on our toes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>These seven long years I've been serving now</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And seven long more have to stay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All for bashing a bloke down our alley</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And taking his ticker away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, had I the wings of a turtle-dove,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I'd soar on my pinions so high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Straight back to the arms of my Polly love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And in her sweet presence I'd die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now all my young Dookies and Duchesses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Take warning from what I've to say:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Mind all is your own as you toucheses</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or you'll find us in Botany Bay.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botany_Bay_(song)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="TWgnSD27P5E" data-title="Botany Bay: John Williamson"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">John Williamson</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel John Williamson - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TWgnSD27P5E" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Sundowners (film; sung by Deborah Kerr, Robert Mitchum, Peter Ustinov and Glynis Johns)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a verse and chorus sung in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Thorn Birds (television miniseries)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1983</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sleepless (Kate Rusby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><a href="/songs/waltzing-matilda">Waltzing Matilda (Mirusia and André Rieu)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Toorali (The Herd, on Summerland)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted excerpt used for the chorus and main verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Toorali (Eleventh He Reaches London, on Hollow Be My Name)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parts of the chorus used</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fisticuffs (Primus, on Brown Album)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">refers to Botany Bay</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Farewell to Judges and Juries</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1820</span><span class="sg-chain-h">lines in common with the verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mush, Mush</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody appears to have been adapted from it</span></li></ul></section>
